{
  "abstract": "This Lean 4 and Mathlib development produces exact, machine-checked positivity certificates for finite Gram matrices built from Suzuki's zeta screw function. Weil's explicit formula recasts the Riemann Hypothesis as positivity of a quadratic form assembled from prime powers, and a recent line of work truncates that form and studies the resulting finite matrices. The objects formalized here are the screw function Psi, defined as an archimedean profile minus a von Mangoldt weighted prime sum and extended evenly; its Gram kernel G(t,u) = Psi(t) + Psi(u) - Psi(t-u); and positive definiteness, in the quadratic-form sense, of the finite matrices this kernel generates on explicit grids.\nThe three compared results are as follows. (1) On the grid (0.2, 0.4, 0.6) the 3 by 3 Gram matrix M3 of the genuine kernel satisfies an explicit Rayleigh floor: for every real vector x, 0.005 times the sum of the x_i squared is at most the quadratic form of M3 at x. (2) M3 is positive definite. (3) On the grid (0.4, 0.9) the 2 by 2 Gram matrix is positive definite. The prime sum in Psi vanishes for t below log 2 (about 0.693) and the node 0.9 lies above it, so the p = 2 prime term contributes to this matrix.\nThe interest is that the surrounding literature (Connes and van Suijlekom; Connes, Consani and Moscovici; Suzuki) works either in floating point or at the level of existence statements, whereas these are exact certificates checked against the definition of Psi itself. Suzuki establishes that some positivity window exists; here an explicit one is exhibited, with an explicit margin, and verified, to our knowledge for the first time.\nScope is finite and explicit. Every positivity statement concerns a specified grid at specified rational nodes; there is no asymptotic, fine-mesh or all-grid result; and nothing here bears on the Riemann Hypothesis.",
